<!-- cell: title-page id="2e36e60f-f755-408c-93a2-0771d8378298" title="The Weather in the Book" -->

<!-- cell: markdown id="89317317-d57c-4048-9302-57826e08004a" -->

Wren had been apprenticed to Master Corvin for three years, and in three years he had taught her to sweep the tower stairs, grind salt for the wards and copy out the names of clouds. He had not taught her a single spell, and she could think of nothing else.

The tower stood alone on the Saltings, where the marsh met the grey sea, and from its top window Wren could see the village of Ebb two miles inland. That summer no rain had fallen on Ebb for nine weeks. The wells were mud, and the barley stood white in the fields.

"Can't you do something?" she asked. "Weather is not a thing you do," said Corvin, without looking up from his book. "It is a thing you ask, and then you are responsible for the answer."

That night, while he slept, Wren took the Book of Weathers from its chain. She knew where the key was; she had swept around it a thousand times.

At the top of the tower she read the rain-calling aloud, every word, the way she had copied the names of clouds, and felt the words leave her like birds.

The clouds came in from the sea fast and black. The rain fell on Ebb, and Wren laughed out loud. Then it fell harder. The marsh rose around the tower, the river below the village burst its banks, and she watched the lights of Ebb go out one by one under the water.

She tried to unsay the spell, but the book had no unsaying. So she ran down the stairs she had swept so many times and woke her master, and told him everything.

Corvin listened without a word, pulled his coat on over his nightshirt and climbed to the top window to look at the drowning fields. "I can't stop it for you," he said. "It answers the one who asked. But I can tell you the words, if you will say them."

So he spoke the long, old words of thanks and release one at a time, and Wren repeated them into the storm with salt water streaming down her face. The last word was the hardest. It meant, as near as anyone could translate it, enough.

The rain thinned to drizzle, then to mist, and by morning the river had found its banks again and the wells of Ebb were full.

Corvin made two cups of tea and set one in front of her. "Lesson one," he said. "Tomorrow we begin with clouds that only threaten."

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="76436dcb-4492-4f94-9f72-f53d4b99e259" title="Wren" words="0-49, 102-107, 137-164, 165-194, 248-279, 330-358" -->

An impatient, gifted apprentice who has swept stairs for three years and craves real magic. She acts before she understands, and has the courage to own her mistake.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="baab81e9-dc0f-4531-965a-69ddbd17b31e" title="Master Corvin" words="0-34, 108-136, 280-329, 330-358, 400-424" -->

An old weather-wizard, slow-spoken and wise, who teaches that weather is asked, not done. He will not undo his apprentice's spell for her, but gives her the words to undo it herself.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="978ca3b4-1d85-4013-8c79-b44bafe904eb" title="The Saltings" words="0-34, 50-101, 137-164, 165-194, 215-247, 280-303, 375-399" -->

The salt marsh by the grey sea where Corvin's lone tower stands, two miles from the drought-struck village of Ebb that Wren watches from its top window.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="18d40ccd-71f9-4d18-9a97-f8ee21ffc6c4" title="Ambition" words="35-49, 102-107, 137-164, 165-194, 195-247, 248-259" -->

Wren's hunger to do real magic before she is ready, and what it costs the village.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="e25b15ae-e92c-4e47-946e-d8c771bee9f3" title="Humility" words="108-136, 260-279, 304-329, 330-374, 400-424" -->

What Wren learns: to ask for help, to take responsibility, and to say 'enough'.

<!-- cell: plot id="ab6271ab-f070-4a04-bf91-33d33a011665" title="The rain-calling" words="35-49, 79-101, 137-164, 165-194, 195-247, 248-279, 304-329, 330-358, 375-399" -->

During a drought Wren steals the Book of Weathers and calls rain; it floods the village; she wakes Corvin, who gives her the words of release to say herself; the flood recedes and her real lessons begin.
